HYPOTHESIS (proxy-based approximation, not a direct measurement): among the 18 original studies of the Experimental Economics Replication Project (EERP; 16 AER, 2 QJE, 2011-2014) contained in meta-regression_study_data_heterogeneity_in_replication_projects.csv, 100.0% (18/18) were conducted in a lab setting, operationally defined as a non-online experiment (online.o == 0) with 'students' or 'community' subjects; no column describes the original study's setting directly (lab_id and aggregated are missing in 100% of ee rows). 95% CI Wilson [0.824, 1.000]; exact Clopper-Pearson [0.815, 1.000]; at the p=1 boundary the interval is effectively one-sided (one-sided 95% lower bound = 0.05^(1/18) = 0.847). The 'unclassifiable' set is empty (0/18: no online/anyone/mixed values and no nulls), so the lower and upper bounds of the proportion coincide (1.000-1.000); results are identical under D2 (online.o==0: 18/18) and D4 (replication side: 18/18) and nearly identical under the strict D3 (students only: 17/18). Excluding drop=True rows does not change the figure (0 of 18 flagged). Caveats: n=18 (wide CI), unit = one O/R pair per row (equal to 18 unique studies by title + first surname), and the scope is only these 18 EERP original studies, not the general Experimental Economics literature. Descriptive context: 11/18 ran in a US lab and 7/18 in a non-US lab (us_lab.o encodes the lab's country, not lab vs non-lab).